Based on the Microsoft guidance, I agree that we should move away from the current connection-string/SAS-based authentication model. Azure Event Hubs supports Microsoft Entra ID authentication and Azure RBAC, removing the need to maintain shared access keys in the application. Microsoft also recommends granting the narrowest required access, and since our DU workload only needs to publish events, the appropriate Event Hub data-plane permission on the destination side would be Azure Event Hubs Data Sender, scoped as narrowly as possible to the required Event Hub. (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/event-hubs/authorize-access-azure-active-directory)

There is an additional consideration in our case because this is a cross-tenant integration. Microsoft documents a Workload Identity Federation pattern in which a user-assigned managed identity (UAMI) attached to the Azure workload can be trusted by a Microsoft Entra application through a federated identity credential. Microsoft specifically states that the app registration must belong to the same tenant as the managed identity, and when resources in another tenant need to be accessed, the application must be configured as a multitenant application and provisioned into the target tenant. Microsoft also confirms that access to resources in another tenant is supported by this federation pattern, without requiring an application secret. (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/workload-id/workload-identity-federation-config-app-trust-managed-identity)

Based on this, the proposed DU-side authentication flow would be:

DU Function App / publishing workload → User-Assigned Managed Identity → Federated Identity Credential → Multitenant Entra Application → target tenant service principal → Event Hub RBAC

This would allow us to replace the existing Event Hub connection-string/SAS authentication with token-based Entra authentication while avoiding long-lived credentials or client secrets.

